module Date

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Writing a date - the segments and their separators, decided at compile time.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Show(Timestamp)` writes the microseconds, which is the honest default for a newtype over an
   integer and is not what anybody wants to read. This is the other rendering, and it is a formatter
   on `Core/Format/`'s pattern: a record holding a reference to the `Date` and the settings to write
   it under, with a `Show` instance, so `print("{iso8601(d)}\n")` is an ordinary format hole.

   **The settings are fields and not a template string**, which is the whole design and is what
   Analysis-Library.md §3.1 means by "a template language, its parser and a formatter" being a
   text-shaped problem this module should not have. `"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S"` has to be scanned at run
   time on every call, its directives dispatched one at a time, and its mistakes discovered at the
   moment the date is printed. Every setting below is a scalar or a payload-free enum, so a preset is
   a constant, the `match` on each one folds where the format is known, and a format that names a
   segment that does not exist does not exist.

   **The presets are functions rather than constants**, which is the one place this departs from what
   a reader might expect. A formatter holds a reference to its subject, so a settings record without
   one is a second type and every call would name both - `format(d, iso8601Style)` against
   `iso8601(d)`. The preset *is* the common case, so it gets the short spelling, and `dateFormat`
   underneath takes every setting by name for the formats nobody thought of.
-}

{-
   Which of the three orders the date parts go in, or no date at all.

   Three and not every permutation: these are the orders in use, and a fourth would be a format no
   reader has. `NoDate` is what makes a time-only rendering the same record as every other, rather
   than a second formatter with half the fields missing.
-}
pub data DateOrder
  = @value(0) NoDate
  | @value(1) YearMonthDay
  | @value(2) DayMonthYear
  | @value(3) MonthDayYear

-- How far down the time is written. Each step is the one before it plus a field, so a format asks
-- for a precision rather than for a set of parts - which is what every caller actually means.
pub data TimeDetail
  = @value(0) NoTime
  | @value(1) Minutes
  | @value(2) Seconds
  | @value(3) Millis
  | @value(4) Micros

-- The month as `07`, `July` or `Jul`. English only, and deliberately: a localized month name is a
-- table per language and a choice of which, which is a library this is not.
pub data MonthStyle = @value(0) MonthNumber | @value(1) MonthName | @value(2) MonthAbbrev

pub data WeekdayStyle = @value(0) NoWeekday | @value(1) WeekdayName | @value(2) WeekdayAbbrev

{-
   The zone, whose four answers are four different things a format means by one.

   `ZoneOffset` is `+0200` and `ZoneOffsetColon` is `+02:00` - RFC 3339 requires the colon and
   RFC 5322 forbids it, which is why both are here. `ZoneZulu` is `Z` for UTC and the colon form for
   anything else, which is what an ISO-8601 timestamp is normally written as. `ZoneUtcName` is the
   literal `GMT` an HTTP date ends in, and it says nothing about the offset because that format is
   only ever written at zero.
-}
pub data ZoneStyle
  = @value(0) NoZone
  | @value(1) ZoneOffset
  | @value(2) ZoneOffsetColon
  | @value(3) ZoneZulu
  | @value(4) ZoneUtcName

{-
   How to write a date - the settings, with no date in them.

   **A style and not a formatter**, which is the one structural thing to know about this file. The
   settings used to be fields of a record that also held the `Date`, and `readDate` below restated
   all nine as its own parameters: one fact written twice, with nothing to keep the two in step. They
   are one record now, and `Core/Format.yana`'s `Formatted(DateStyle, Date)` is what puts a style back
   together with a date. `ShowWith` reads it and `ReadWith` reads the same one, so a round trip
   through a preset is the identity by construction rather than by promise.

   Every field is a scalar or a payload-free enum, which is not only about folding: provenance is
   field-insensitive, so an aggregate argument reaching a record that holds a reference is read as a
   root of it. A style of scalars is copied into the wrapper and roots nothing, which is what lets
   the constructors below build their own style and hand the result back.

   The two separators are `CodeUnit`s rather than strings for the same reason and one more: every
   separator any of these formats uses is one unit, and a zero means none - which is what the compact
   ISO form `20260829T120000Z` is, and it costs no extra field to say.
-}
pub data DateStyle {
    order: DateOrder = YearMonthDay,
    dateSeparator: CodeUnit = 45,
    month: MonthStyle = MonthNumber,
    weekday: WeekdayStyle = NoWeekday,
    between: CodeUnit = 84,
    time: TimeDetail = Seconds,
    timeSeparator: CodeUnit = 58,
    zone: ZoneStyle = NoZone
}

-- A date under a style, which is what a format hole is handed. The name is what this type was called
-- when it held the settings itself, and every signature below still reads as it did.
pub alias DateFormat = Formatted(DateStyle, Date)

-- Every setting by name, for a format the presets below do not cover. The defaults are ISO-8601's,
-- so what a caller writes is the difference from it rather than the whole of a format.
pub fn dateStyle(order: DateOrder = YearMonthDay, dateSeparator: CodeUnit = 45,
                 month: MonthStyle = MonthNumber, weekday: WeekdayStyle = NoWeekday,
                 between: CodeUnit = 84, time: TimeDetail = Seconds,
                 timeSeparator: CodeUnit = 58, zone: ZoneStyle = NoZone) -> DateStyle =
    DateStyle {order: order, dateSeparator: dateSeparator, month: month, weekday: weekday,
               between: between, time: time, timeSeparator: timeSeparator, zone: zone}


{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The presets
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The formats a program actually writes, each named after the document that defines it so that a
   reader can check one against the other.

   **A style apiece, and each says only its difference from the one it derives from.** These used to
   be six formatter constructors and six readers, twelve functions restating field values by hand -
   and `iso8601Compact` named `zone: ZoneZulu` on its own where `rfc3339` also set it, two places
   free to disagree. A record update names the base and the difference, so the chain is the
   documentation: RFC 3339 is ISO 8601 with a zone, and the compact form is RFC 3339 with the
   separators dropped. Every one of them folds to a constant at a call site that uses it.
-}

-- `2026-08-29T12:00:00`, which is ISO 8601's extended form with no zone - what a log line wants.
pub fn isoStyle() -> DateStyle = DateStyle {}

-- `2026-08-29T12:00:00Z`, or `+02:00` where the date carries an offset. RFC 3339 §5.6.
pub fn rfc3339Style() -> DateStyle = {isoStyle() | zone: ZoneZulu}

-- `20260829T120000Z` - the same instant with every separator dropped, which is ISO 8601's basic
-- form and what a file name wants. RFC 3339's zone, said once, up there.
pub fn iso8601CompactStyle() -> DateStyle =
    {rfc3339Style() | dateSeparator: 0, timeSeparator: 0}

-- `Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:00:00 GMT` - RFC 7231 §7.1.1.1's one legal HTTP date, which is RFC 5322's
-- with the zone fixed and the day always two digits.
pub fn httpStyle() -> DateStyle =
    {isoStyle() | order: DayMonthYear, dateSeparator: 32, month: MonthAbbrev,
                  weekday: WeekdayAbbrev, between: 32, zone: ZoneUtcName}

-- `2026-08-29` and `12:00:00`, which are the two halves asked for on their own often enough to name.
pub fn dateOnlyStyle() -> DateStyle = {isoStyle() | time: NoTime}
pub fn timeOnlyStyle() -> DateStyle = {isoStyle() | order: NoDate}

{-
   And the six applied to a date, under the `Date` namespace.

   `Date.http(d)` rather than a bare `http`, because these are the one family here whose names would
   be ambiguous unqualified: `compact`, `http` and `dateOnly` say nothing about dates on their own,
   where `hex` and `padded` in `Core/Format/` say what they format. A namespace is what the language
   already has for that - the same one `String.push` is declared in - and it costs a call site the
   four units that make it read.

   **Each writes the record literal itself rather than calling `with`.** `with` takes its style as
   `'s`, so a function that built its own style and handed a `with` result back would be returning a
   root into its own frame; at the concrete `DateStyle` the copy is a copy and there is no loan.
   `Core/Format.yana` states the rule and this is the file it points at.
-}
-- Every setting by name, for a format the presets do not cover - `dateStyle`'s arguments, applied.
pub fn Date.format(value: 'Date, order: DateOrder = YearMonthDay, dateSeparator: CodeUnit = 45,
                   month: MonthStyle = MonthNumber, weekday: WeekdayStyle = NoWeekday,
                   between: CodeUnit = 84, time: TimeDetail = Seconds,
                   timeSeparator: CodeUnit = 58, zone: ZoneStyle = NoZone) -> DateFormat =
    Formatted {value: value, style: dateStyle(order, dateSeparator, month, weekday, between,
                                              time, timeSeparator, zone)}

pub fn Date.iso8601(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = Formatted {value: value, style: isoStyle()}
pub fn Date.rfc3339(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = Formatted {value: value, style: rfc3339Style()}

pub fn Date.compact(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat =
    Formatted {value: value, style: iso8601CompactStyle()}

pub fn Date.http(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = Formatted {value: value, style: httpStyle()}
pub fn Date.dateOnly(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = Formatted {value: value, style: dateOnlyStyle()}
pub fn Date.timeOnly(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = Formatted {value: value, style: timeOnlyStyle()}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Writing one
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-}

-- The English month names, as one function rather than a table: `nameOf` generates exactly this for
-- an enum and a month is a number here rather than a constructor, so the chain is written out. It
-- folds to one string wherever the month is known and is a jump table where it is not.
fn monthName(month: Int) -> String = match month:
    1 -> "January"
    2 -> "February"
    3 -> "March"
    4 -> "April"
    5 -> "May"
    6 -> "June"
    7 -> "July"
    8 -> "August"
    9 -> "September"
    10 -> "October"
    11 -> "November"
    12 -> "December"
    _ -> "?"

-- Three units of the name above, which is what every abbreviation in use is - and is why this is a
-- slice of the one table rather than a second one to keep in step with it.
fn monthAbbrev(month: Int) -> String = String.slice(monthName(month), 0, 3)

fn weekdayName(day: Weekday) -> String = nameOf(day)
fn weekdayAbbrev(day: Weekday) -> String = String.slice(nameOf(day), 0, 3)

-- A field at a fixed width, which every part of a date but the year is. `number` is
-- `Core/Format/Number.yana`'s, so the zero padding is one setting rather than a branch here.
--
-- At `Long` and not at `Int`, because the parts arrive at the widths `Date` stores them in - a `U8`
-- hour, a `U32` micros - and `U32` does not fit an `I32`. One widening rung serves all of them,
-- where a parameter at each part's own type would be six bodies saying the same line.
fn pushFixed(value: Long, digits: Size, &to: String):
    show(with(value, numberStyle(digits: digits)), to)

-- The year, which is the one part whose width is not fixed and the one that can be negative. Four
-- digits is ISO 8601's minimum and a year outside it simply takes the room it needs.
fn pushYear(year: I32, &to: String): show(with(year, numberStyle(digits: 4)), to)

fn pushSeparator(unit: CodeUnit, &to: String):
    if unit != 0 then String.pushUnit(to, unit :: Int)

{-
   The three parts a date is made of, and the order a setting puts them in.

   One `match` answering *which* parts, and one body writing a part - rather than three arms each
   transcribing the same three writes and the two separators between them. The orders differ in
   nothing but sequence, so sequence is the only thing worth saying three times.
-}
data DatePart = @value(0) YearPart | @value(1) MonthPart | @value(2) DayPart

fn datePartsOf(order: DateOrder) -> {DatePart, DatePart, DatePart} = match order:
    NoDate -> {YearPart, MonthPart, DayPart}
    YearMonthDay -> {YearPart, MonthPart, DayPart}
    DayMonthYear -> {DayPart, MonthPart, YearPart}
    MonthDayYear -> {MonthPart, DayPart, YearPart}

fn pushDatePiece(style: DateStyle, date: Date, part: DatePart, &to: String):
    match part:
        YearPart: pushYear(date.year, to)
        MonthPart: pushMonth(style, date, to)
        DayPart: pushFixed(widen(date.day) :: Long, 2, to)

fn pushDatePart(style: DateStyle, date: Date, &to: String):
    if style.order is NoDate then return

    let {first, second, third} = datePartsOf(style.order)

    pushDatePiece(style, date, first, to)
    pushSeparator(style.dateSeparator, to)
    pushDatePiece(style, date, second, to)
    pushSeparator(style.dateSeparator, to)
    pushDatePiece(style, date, third, to)

fn pushMonth(style: DateStyle, date: Date, &to: String):
    let month = date.month :: Int

    match style.month:
        MonthNumber: pushFixed(widen(month) :: Long, 2, to)
        MonthName: String.push(to, monthName(month))
        MonthAbbrev: String.push(to, monthAbbrev(month))

fn pushTimePart(style: DateStyle, date: Date, &to: String):
    if style.time is NoTime then return

    pushFixed(widen(date.hour) :: Long, 2, to)
    pushSeparator(style.timeSeparator, to)
    pushFixed(widen(date.minute) :: Long, 2, to)

    if style.time is Minutes then return

    pushSeparator(style.timeSeparator, to)
    pushFixed(widen(date.second) :: Long, 2, to)

    -- The fraction, whose separator is a point in every format that has one.
    match style.time:
        Millis:
            String.pushUnit(to, 46)
            pushFixed((widen(date.micros) :: Long) / 1000, 3, to)

        Micros:
            String.pushUnit(to, 46)
            pushFixed(widen(date.micros) :: Long, 6, to)

        _: {}

fn pushZonePart(style: DateStyle, date: Date, &to: String):
    let minutes = date.zoneMinutes :: Int

    match style.zone:
        NoZone: {}

        ZoneUtcName: String.push(to, " GMT")

        ZoneZulu:
            if minutes == 0 then String.pushUnit(to, 90)
            else pushOffset(minutes, True, to)

        ZoneOffset: pushOffset(minutes, False, to)
        ZoneOffsetColon: pushOffset(minutes, True, to)

-- `+02:00` or `-0530`. The sign is the offset's own and is written for zero too, which is what every
-- format that asks for an offset rather than for `Z` expects.
fn pushOffset(minutes: Int, colon: Bool, &to: String):
    if minutes < 0 then String.pushUnit(to, 45) else String.pushUnit(to, 43)

    let size = if minutes < 0 then -minutes else minutes
    pushFixed((widen(size) :: Long) / 60, 2, to)
    if colon then String.pushUnit(to, 58)
    pushFixed((widen(size) :: Long) % 60, 2, to)

instance ShowWith(DateStyle, Date):
    fn showWith(subject: DateFormat, &to: String):
        let style = subject.style :: DateStyle
        let date = subject.value :: Date

        -- The weekday comes off the date rather than out of it, which is why it is not a field of
        -- `Date`: it is a function of the other three and storing it would be a fourth thing to keep
        -- in step. `weekdayOf` is that function.
        match style.weekday:
            NoWeekday: {}

            WeekdayName:
                String.push(to, weekdayName(weekdayOf(date)))
                String.push(to, ", ")

            WeekdayAbbrev:
                String.push(to, weekdayAbbrev(weekdayOf(date)))
                String.push(to, ", ")

        pushDatePart(style, date, to)

        -- The unit between the two halves is written only where there are two halves to put it
        -- between, which is what makes `dateOnly` and `timeOnly` the same style as the rest.
        if !(style.order is NoDate) && !(style.time is NoTime) then pushSeparator(style.between, to)

        pushTimePart(style, date, to)
        pushZonePart(style, date, to)

    {-
       The bound, which is generous and constant.

       Every part has a width the settings fix, and the widest reading of each is what this adds up:
       a weekday name and its comma and space (11), a year (11 - `Show(I32)`'s own bound), two
       separators (2), a month name (9), a day (2), the unit between (1), a time with microseconds
       (15), and a zone (6). That is 57, rounded up to 64 so that no future segment silently makes it
       wrong.

       A constant rather than a sum over the settings, deliberately. The settings are known at every
       call site that uses a preset, so a computed bound would fold to a number anyway - and what it
       would cost is a `showBound` that has to be kept in step with `show` field by field. Sixty-four
       units is one cache line and less than any date's text is worth arguing about.
    -}
    fn showBoundWith(subject: DateFormat) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(64)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `IntervalFormat` - a duration as a human reads one
   ==========================================================================================

   `Show(Interval)` writes the microseconds, which is the right default for the same reason
   `Show(Timestamp)` is and is unreadable at every scale a human uses. This is the readable form, and
   it is two renderings rather than one:

   - the **clock** form, `1h23m4.5s`, which drops the parts that are zero from the *left*, so the
     units present are the ones the duration actually has;
   - a **scaled** form, `1.5ms`, which is one number and the unit it is counted in.

   **The default picks between them on the magnitude**, which is the whole reason this file changed:
   the clock form is the right rendering of a minute and a nonsense rendering of a millisecond, and
   it used to be the only one - so `1500` microseconds printed as `1s` and everything below a second
   printed as `0s`. A minute is where the clock starts earning its keep, so that is where it starts:
   anything at or above one is a clock, and anything below it is scaled to the largest of
   nanoseconds, microseconds, milliseconds and seconds it reaches. Zero is a clock, because `0s` is
   what a duration of nothing should say and `0ns` is a precision it does not have.

   **The places follow the same rule `bytes` uses**, and for the same reason: one decimal where the
   number is below ten, none at or above it, and none at all where the unit is the one the value is
   already counted in. So `1500` microseconds is `1.5ms`, `11500` is `11ms`, and `999` is `999us`
   rather than `999.0us`.

   Both halves are overridable and neither is a mode: `duration(d, unit: TimeUnit.Millis)` always
   writes milliseconds, `duration(d, unit: TimeUnit.Clock)` always writes the clock, and
   `duration(d, decimals: 3)` always writes three places. The clock form's own places are the
   fraction of its seconds, which is what they always were - `duration(millis(4500), decimals: 3)` is
   `4.500s`.

   **The largest unit is a day and the smallest a nanosecond.** Above a day the answer stops being
   arithmetic - a month is not a fixed number of them - and an `Interval` is a duration rather than a
   span between two dates, so a month is a thing it cannot mean. Below a nanosecond there is no
   clock that measures.
-}

{-
   Which unit a duration is written in.

   `@value` is declaration order here rather than a number the arithmetic reads, because the
   arithmetic is a *ratio* between two of these and a ratio is not a value either of them has -
   `nanosPer` below is the table, and it is one function rather than seven multipliers spread through
   the two directions. What the order does buy is that the units are comparable, which is what "the
   largest unit this value reaches" means.

   `Clock` and `Auto` are last and are not units at all: one is the other rendering and one is the
   choice between them. They are constructors rather than a second field for `ByteScale`'s reason -
   a style is a record of scalars, and one more constructor reads better at a call site than a
   wrapper at the field.
-}
pub data qualified TimeUnit
  = @value(0) Nanos
  | @value(1) Micros
  | @value(2) Millis
  | @value(3) Seconds
  | @value(4) Minutes
  | @value(5) Hours
  | @value(6) Days
  | @value(7) Clock
  | @value(8) Auto

{-
   How to write a duration.

   `decimals` is a `Size` with `-1` for "decide from the value", which is `ByteStyle`'s sentinel and
   is spent for its reason: zero places is a real request, so the absent value has to be a number
   below the range rather than the bottom of it. It used to default to `0`, which was the same
   number as "none" and is why there was nothing for a scaled form to key on.
-}
pub data IntervalStyle {
    unit: TimeUnit = TimeUnit.Auto,
    decimals: Size = -1
}

pub alias IntervalFormat = Formatted(IntervalStyle, Interval)

{-
   A plain count of nanoseconds, under the same style and through the same body.

   `Interval` is microseconds, so a nanosecond count is not one - `monotonicNanos` answers an `I64`
   and `Date.yana`'s `nanos` constructor divides it away. What a benchmark has is that `I64`, and
   what it wants is `47ns` rather than `0us`, so the subject here is the number itself and the style
   is told nothing: which unit the value is counted in is the *formatter's* to know, and it is the
   one thing that differs between the two.

   `I64` and not a newtype over it, because there is nothing to protect: a nanosecond count is a
   number, the formatter names the unit, and a wrapper would be a second `Interval` with the same
   argument against it.
-}
pub alias NanosFormat = Formatted(IntervalStyle, I64)

{-
   The styles and the formatters, which is what a call site writes.

   `durationStyle` keeps its name and its meaning - a clock, with the places being the fraction of
   its seconds - because that is what every caller of it means and what `readDuration` reads.
   `intervalStyle` is the general one and defaults to picking.
-}
pub fn intervalStyle(unit: TimeUnit = TimeUnit.Auto, decimals: Size = -1) -> IntervalStyle =
    IntervalStyle {unit: unit, decimals: decimals}

pub fn durationStyle(decimals: Size = 0) -> IntervalStyle =
    IntervalStyle {unit: TimeUnit.Clock, decimals: decimals}

pub fn duration(value: 'Interval, unit: TimeUnit = TimeUnit.Auto,
                decimals: Size = -1) -> IntervalFormat =
    Formatted {value: value, style: intervalStyle(unit, decimals)}

pub fn nanoseconds(value: 'I64, unit: TimeUnit = TimeUnit.Auto,
                   decimals: Size = -1) -> NanosFormat =
    Formatted {value: value, style: intervalStyle(unit, decimals)}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The two renderings, written once and shared by the two subjects
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The body below takes the magnitude, its sign and the unit the magnitude is *counted in*, which is
   the one thing an `Interval` and a nanosecond count disagree about. Everything else - picking a
   unit, picking the places, the digits and the clock - is the same code for both.
-}

-- How many nanoseconds one of each unit is. The table, so that the ratio between two units is one
-- division and there is no second list of multipliers to drift from this one. `Clock` and `Auto`
-- answer a second, which is the threshold the picker compares against and is never used as a
-- divisor.
fn nanosPer(unit: TimeUnit) -> I64 = match unit:
    TimeUnit.Nanos -> 1
    TimeUnit.Micros -> 1000
    TimeUnit.Millis -> 1000000
    TimeUnit.Seconds -> 1000000000
    TimeUnit.Minutes -> 60000000000
    TimeUnit.Hours -> 3600000000000
    TimeUnit.Days -> 86400000000000
    TimeUnit.Clock -> 1000000000
    TimeUnit.Auto -> 1000000000

-- And what each one is spelled. `us` and not `µs`, because a `µ` is two UTF-8 bytes natively and one
-- code unit on JS - so a column of durations would come out a different width on the two targets,
-- which is `Format/Field.yana`'s reason for `...` said about a different character.
fn timeSuffix(unit: TimeUnit) -> String = match unit:
    TimeUnit.Nanos -> "ns"
    TimeUnit.Micros -> "us"
    TimeUnit.Millis -> "ms"
    TimeUnit.Seconds -> "s"
    TimeUnit.Minutes -> "m"
    TimeUnit.Hours -> "h"
    TimeUnit.Days -> "d"
    TimeUnit.Clock -> "s"
    TimeUnit.Auto -> "s"

{-
   The clock, `1h23m4.500s` - which is what this file wrote before there was anything else, moved
   into a function so that both subjects reach it.

   The magnitude arrives in microseconds because that is what the arithmetic below divides, and the
   sign is separate because it belongs in front of the whole rendering rather than to any one
   component.
-}
fn showClock(micros: I64, negative: Bool, decimals: Size, &to: String):
    if negative then String.pushUnit(to, 45)

    let days = micros / 86400000000
    let hours = (micros / 3600000000) % 24
    let minutes = (micros / 60000000) % 60
    let seconds = (micros / 1000000) % 60
    let fraction = micros % 1000000

    -- Each unit is written once something above it has been, so the leading zeros go and the ones
    -- inside stay: `1h0m4s` keeps its minutes because its hours were written.
    let &started = False

    if days != 0:
        show(days, to)
        String.pushUnit(to, 100)
        started = True

    if started || hours != 0:
        show(hours, to)
        String.pushUnit(to, 104)
        started = True

    if started || minutes != 0:
        show(minutes, to)
        String.pushUnit(to, 109)

    show(seconds, to)

    if decimals > 0:
        String.pushUnit(to, 46)

        -- The fraction, truncated to the places asked for rather than rounded: a duration that
        -- reads as shorter than it is, is the direction a reader can act on.
        let &scaled = fraction
        let &left = 6 - (widen(decimals) :: Long)

        while left > 0:
            scaled = scaled / 10
            left = left - 1

        show(with(scaled, numberStyle(digits: decimals)), to)

    String.pushUnit(to, 115)

{-
   And the scaled form, which is one number and a unit.

   The rounding and the place count are `Format/Bytes.yana`'s, down to the halving loop that keeps
   the multiply inside the width - see the block there, which states why dividing the divisor down
   first is wrong and why the rounding is a comparison rather than an addition. A duration and a byte
   count are the same arithmetic over a different table of divisors.

   The one place they differ is the rounding *direction* at the clock form above, which truncates
   deliberately; a scaled duration rounds, because `1.9995s` reading as `1.9s` is the error a
   benchmark result cannot afford and there is no honest argument for a shorter answer at three
   significant figures.
-}
fn showScaled(size: I64, negative: Bool, divisor: I64, suffix: String, decimals: Size, &to: String):
    if negative then String.pushUnit(to, 45)

    let &whole = size / divisor
    let rest = size % divisor

    let wanted = if:
        decimals >= 0             -> decimals
        divisor > 1 && whole < 10 -> 1
        else                      -> 0

    let &power = 1 :: I64
    let &places = 0 :: Size

    while places < wanted && power <= divisor / 10:
        power = power * 10
        places = places + 1

    let &frac = 0 :: I64

    if places > 0:
        let &num = rest
        let &den = divisor

        while num > (maxValue() :: I64) / power:
            num = num / 2
            den = den / 2

        let scaled = num * power
        let quotient = scaled / den
        let left = scaled % den
        let rounded = if left >= den - left then quotient + 1 else quotient

        if rounded >= power:
            whole = whole + 1
            frac = 0
        else frac = rounded

    show(whole, to)

    if places > 0:
        String.pushUnit(to, 46)

        let &at = power / 10
        while at > 0:
            String.pushUnit(to, 48 + (truncate((frac / at) % 10) :: Int))
            at = at / 10

    String.push(to, suffix)

{-
   Which unit an automatic rendering picks.

   Largest first, and never below the one the value is counted in - which is what the divisions in
   the tests say: `1000000 / sourceNanos` is how many source units a millisecond is, and a source
   coarser than a millisecond would make it zero. The two sources this file has are nanoseconds and
   microseconds, so it cannot; a third, coarser one would need the test written the other way up.

   Zero is a clock rather than the finest unit, because `0s` is what a duration of nothing says and
   `0ns` claims a precision it does not have.
-}
fn autoUnit(size: I64, source: TimeUnit) -> TimeUnit:
    let sourceNanos = nanosPer(source)

    if size == 0 || size >= 60000000000 / sourceNanos then return TimeUnit.Clock
    if size >= 1000000000 / sourceNanos then return TimeUnit.Seconds
    if size >= 1000000 / sourceNanos then return TimeUnit.Millis
    if size >= 1000 / sourceNanos then return TimeUnit.Micros
    return source

-- The whole of both instances, with the source unit the only argument that differs between them.
fn showDuration(total: I64, source: TimeUnit, style: IntervalStyle, &to: String):
    let negative = total < 0
    let size = if negative then -total else total

    let unit = if style.unit is TimeUnit.Auto then autoUnit(size, source) else style.unit
    let sourceNanos = nanosPer(source)

    if unit is TimeUnit.Clock:
        -- The clock divides microseconds, so a nanosecond count is brought up to them first. Its
        -- places are the fraction of a second and default to none, which is what `durationStyle`
        -- has always meant.
        let asMicros = size / (1000 / sourceNanos)
        showClock(asMicros, negative, if style.decimals >= 0 then style.decimals else 0, to)
        return

    -- A unit below the one the value is counted in has no divisor - a nanosecond of a microsecond
    -- count is not a number the value holds - so it is read as the source unit itself.
    let target = if nanosPer(unit) < sourceNanos then source else unit
    let divisor = nanosPer(target) / sourceNanos

    showScaled(size, negative, divisor, timeSuffix(target), style.decimals, to)

instance ShowWith(IntervalStyle, Interval):
    fn showWith(subject: IntervalFormat, &to: String):
        showDuration(toMicros(subject.value), TimeUnit.Micros, subject.style, to)

    -- Twenty for the days, two units apiece for the three unit letters and their numbers, a point
    -- and the places after it. Generous, and constant for the same reason `DateFormat`'s is; the
    -- places are the style's where it names one and one where it does not.
    fn showBoundWith(subject: IntervalFormat) -> Maybe(Size) =
        Just(40 + (if subject.style.decimals > 0 then subject.style.decimals else 1))

instance ShowWith(IntervalStyle, I64):
    fn showWith(subject: NanosFormat, &to: String):
        showDuration(subject.value, TimeUnit.Nanos, subject.style, to)

    fn showBoundWith(subject: NanosFormat) -> Maybe(Size) =
        Just(40 + (if subject.style.decimals > 0 then subject.style.decimals else 1))

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Reading a date back - the other direction of everything above.
   ==========================================================================================

   The dual of `dateFormat` argument for argument: the same nine settings say what the text looks
   like, and what comes back is an owned `Date` rather than a formatter over one. A reader has no
   subject to borrow, which is the whole difference between the two directions - see the same
   paragraph in `Core/Format/Number.yana`, whose readers these are written on the model of.

   **This is a reader for the shapes this file writes, and not a date parser.** `readIso8601` reads
   what `iso8601` wrote; `readDate` with settings reads what `dateFormat` with those settings wrote.
   A text of unknown shape is a different problem - it needs a grammar and a list of formats to try -
   and a caller who has one can try these in turn, since a failed read leaves the cursor where it
   was.

   **Where it is lenient, it is lenient on purpose and in one direction only.** A field written with
   a separator between its parts may be read with fewer digits than were written (`2026-8-9`), since
   nothing is ambiguous there; a compact format has no separators, so its widths are exact and a
   short field is an error. A zone is accepted in any of the spellings this file writes whichever one
   was asked for, because they are distinguishable by their first unit and refusing `Z` where `+0000`
   was expected would be pedantry rather than checking.

   **A negative year is refused**, which is the one thing the writer can produce and this cannot: the
   sign and the date separator are the same unit, so `-2026-08-29` has two readings and neither of
   them is more right than the other. Years before 1 AD are outside every format here in practice.
-}

-- Up to `digits` decimal digits, or exactly that many when `exact` - which is what a format with no
-- separators needs, since nothing else says where a field ends. Nothing is consumed on a failure.
fn readFieldDigits(text: String, &at: Size, digits: Size, exact: Bool) -> Result(ReadError, Long):
    let units = length(text)
    let &cursor = at
    let &value = 0 :: Long
    let &seen = 0 :: Size

    while seen < digits && cursor < units && isDigit(String.unit(text, cursor)):
        value = value * 10 + (widen(String.unit(text, cursor) - 48) :: Long)
        cursor = cursor + 1
        seen = seen + 1

    if seen == 0 then return Err(NoDigits)
    if exact && seen != digits then return Err(NoDigits)

    at = cursor
    return Ok(value)

-- A separator the format says is there. A zero unit is a format that writes none, so there is
-- nothing to expect and nothing to consume.
fn expectUnit(text: String, &at: Size, unit: CodeUnit) -> Result(ReadError, {}):
    if unit == 0 then return Ok({})
    if at >= length(text) || String.unit(text, at) != (unit :: Int) then return Err(NoDigits)

    at = at + 1
    return Ok({})

-- Whether `word` stands at this position, compared without case - which is what lets `JUL` and `jul`
-- read as the month `Jul` was written as.
fn matchesAt(text: String, at: Size, word: String) -> Bool:
    let units = length(word)
    if at + units > length(text) then return False

    for i in 0 .. units:
        if toLowerUnit(String.unit(text, at + i)) != toLowerUnit(String.unit(word, i)) then return False

    return True

{-
   The month, in whichever of the three spellings the format writes.

   The two name forms are a search over the twelve rather than a table lookup, because the names are
   what `monthName` above already answers and a second list here would be a second thing to keep in
   step. Twelve comparisons on a date parse is nothing worth arranging around, and the full name is
   tried before the abbreviation so that `June` does not read as `Jun` with an `e` left over.
-}
fn readMonthField(text: String, &at: Size, style: MonthStyle, separator: CodeUnit) -> Result(ReadError, Long):
    match style:
        MonthNumber: return readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)

        _:
            let &month = 1 :: Int

            while month <= 12:
                let full = monthName(month)
                let short = monthAbbrev(month)

                if matchesAt(text, at, full):
                    at = at + length(full)
                    return Ok(widen(month) :: Long)

                if matchesAt(text, at, short):
                    at = at + length(short)
                    return Ok(widen(month) :: Long)

                month = month + 1

            return Err(NoDigits)

-- The weekday a format writes in front of the date, which is a function of the date itself - so it
-- is read past rather than read: whatever it says, the date decides what day it was. The comma and
-- the space after it go with it.
fn skipWeekday(text: String, &at: Size):
    let units = length(text)
    let &cursor = at

    while cursor < units && isAlpha(String.unit(text, cursor)):
        cursor = cursor + 1

    if cursor == at then return

    while cursor < units && (String.unit(text, cursor) == 44 || String.unit(text, cursor) == 32):
        cursor = cursor + 1

    at = cursor

{-
   The zone, in any of the spellings this file writes.

   `Z`, `GMT` and `UTC` are zero; `+HH:MM` and `+HHMM` are their minutes, and the colon is optional
   in both directions. A leading space is consumed because `ZoneUtcName` writes one - and consuming
   it before a `+` costs nothing, since no format here writes a space before an offset.
-}
fn readZoneField(text: String, &at: Size, style: ZoneStyle) -> Result(ReadError, I16):
    if style is NoZone then return Ok(0)

    let units = length(text)
    let &cursor = at

    if cursor < units && String.unit(text, cursor) == 32 then cursor = cursor + 1
    if cursor >= units then return Err(NoDigits)

    let unit = toLowerUnit(String.unit(text, cursor))

    if unit == 122:
        at = cursor + 1
        return Ok(0)

    if matchesAt(text, cursor, "gmt") || matchesAt(text, cursor, "utc"):
        at = cursor + 3
        return Ok(0)

    if unit != 43 && unit != 45 then return Err(NoDigits)

    let negative = unit == 45
    cursor = cursor + 1

    let hours = readFieldDigits(text, cursor, 2, True)?
    if cursor < units && String.unit(text, cursor) == 58 then cursor = cursor + 1
    let minutes = readFieldDigits(text, cursor, 2, True)?

    let total = hours * 60 + minutes
    if total > 1440 then return Err(OutOfRange)

    at = cursor
    return Ok(truncate(if negative then -total else total) :: I16)

-- One of the three date pieces, written into the date being built. The ranges are checked here
-- rather than left to `timestampOf`, which deliberately accepts month 13 as January of the next
-- year: that is right for arithmetic and wrong for an input, where a number outside the calendar is
-- the input's mistake and `OutOfRange` is what says so.
fn readDatePiece(text: String, &at: Size, part: DatePart, style: MonthStyle,
                 separator: CodeUnit, &date: Date) -> Result(ReadError, {}):
    match part:
        YearPart:
            let year = readFieldDigits(text, at, if separator == 0 then 4 else 9, separator == 0)?
            date.year = truncate(year) :: I32

        MonthPart:
            let month = readMonthField(text, at, style, separator)?
            if month < 1 || month > 12 then return Err(OutOfRange)
            date.month = truncate(month) :: U8

        DayPart:
            let day = readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)?
            if day < 1 || day > 31 then return Err(OutOfRange)
            date.day = truncate(day) :: U8

    return Ok({})

{-
   The time, to the precision the format says it was written at.

   Read as the writer writes it, step for step: an hour and a minute, then a second where the detail
   asks for one, then a fraction where it asks for that. The fraction is read as up to six digits and
   scaled to microseconds by how many arrived, so `.5` is half a second and `.500` is the same
   number - which is what makes a `Millis` format and a `Micros` one readable by one body.

   A second of 60 is accepted, which is the one place this is looser than the writer: a leap second
   is written by other people's formatters and refusing to read one would be refusing a real input.
-}
fn readTimePart(text: String, &at: Size, detail: TimeDetail, separator: CodeUnit,
                &date: Date) -> Result(ReadError, {}):
    if detail is NoTime then return Ok({})

    let hour = readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)?
    expectUnit(text, at, separator)?
    let minute = readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)?

    if hour > 23 || minute > 59 then return Err(OutOfRange)

    date.hour = truncate(hour) :: U8
    date.minute = truncate(minute) :: U8

    if detail is Minutes then return Ok({})

    expectUnit(text, at, separator)?
    let second = readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)?
    if second > 60 then return Err(OutOfRange)
    date.second = truncate(second) :: U8

    -- The fraction is read wherever there are seconds to attach it to, and not only where the
    -- setting asks for one - which is the second place this is looser than the writer and is worth
    -- the difference: `Seconds` does not write a fraction, and a timestamp another system wrote at
    -- millisecond precision is one this should read rather than stop in the middle of.
    readFraction(text, at, date)?
    return Ok({})

-- The sub-second part, whose separator is a point in every format that has one. Absent is not an
-- error: a whole number of seconds is a legal reading of a format that admits a fraction.
fn readFraction(text: String, &at: Size, &date: Date) -> Result(ReadError, {}):
    if at >= length(text) || String.unit(text, at) != 46 then return Ok({})

    let &cursor = at + 1
    let digits = readFieldDigits(text, cursor, 6, False)?
    let seen = cursor - (at + 1)

    let &micros = digits
    let &left = 6 - seen

    while left > 0:
        micros = micros * 10
        left = left - 1

    date.micros = truncate(micros) :: U32
    at = cursor
    return Ok({})

{-
   A date in the format a style describes - `ShowWith(DateStyle, Date)` above, read rather than
   written.

   **The same style record drives both directions**, which is what makes a preset a round trip rather
   than a promise about one: `readWith(httpStyle(), ...)` reads exactly what `httpDate` writes,
   because there is one `httpStyle` and both of them call it. Every setting this reads is a field the
   writer wrote from.

   The cursor advances only on success, so a caller may try `readRfc3339` and then `readHttpDate`
   against the same position and lose nothing by the first one failing. That is `Core/Read.yana`'s
   contract and every reader in this file keeps it.
-}
instance ReadWith(DateStyle, Date):
    fn readWith(style: DateStyle, from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date):
        let &cursor = at
        let &date = Date {year: 1970, month: 1, day: 1}

        if !(style.weekday is NoWeekday) then skipWeekday(from, cursor)

        if !(style.order is NoDate):
            let {first, second, third} = datePartsOf(style.order)

            readDatePiece(from, cursor, first, style.month, style.dateSeparator, date)?
            expectUnit(from, cursor, style.dateSeparator)?
            readDatePiece(from, cursor, second, style.month, style.dateSeparator, date)?
            expectUnit(from, cursor, style.dateSeparator)?
            readDatePiece(from, cursor, third, style.month, style.dateSeparator, date)?

        if !(style.order is NoDate) && !(style.time is NoTime) then
            expectUnit(from, cursor, style.between)?

        readTimePart(from, cursor, style.time, style.timeSeparator, date)?
        date.zoneMinutes = readZoneField(from, cursor, style.zone)?

        at = cursor
        return Ok(date)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The typed readers, paired with the writers above
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `readWith(httpStyle(), text, at)` is the mechanism and this is its name. `ReadWith(s, a)` has no
   dependency from the style to the value - one `NumberStyle` reads at twelve types - so a bare
   `match readWith(httpStyle(), text, at):` has nothing to infer `Date` from and needs an ascription
   longer than the call. Each of these says it once, in the name, and each is the exact inverse of
   the writer directly above it because both name the same style.

   Namespaced like the writers, and for the same reason: `readCompact` and `readHttp` say nothing
   about dates on their own.
-}
pub fn Date.readFormat(from: String, &at: Size, order: DateOrder = YearMonthDay,
                       dateSeparator: CodeUnit = 45, month: MonthStyle = MonthNumber,
                       weekday: WeekdayStyle = NoWeekday, between: CodeUnit = 84,
                       time: TimeDetail = Seconds, timeSeparator: CodeUnit = 58,
                       zone: ZoneStyle = NoZone) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readWith(dateStyle(order, dateSeparator, month, weekday, between, time, timeSeparator, zone),
             from, at)

pub fn Date.readIso8601(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readWith(isoStyle(), from, at)

pub fn Date.readRfc3339(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readWith(rfc3339Style(), from, at)

pub fn Date.readCompact(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readWith(iso8601CompactStyle(), from, at)

pub fn Date.readHttp(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readWith(httpStyle(), from, at)

pub fn Date.readDateOnly(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readWith(dateOnlyStyle(), from, at)

pub fn Date.readTimeOnly(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readWith(timeOnlyStyle(), from, at)

-- `duration` unqualified, for the reason `hex` is: the name says what it reads.
pub fn readDuration(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Interval) =
    readWith(durationStyle(), from, at)

{-
   And the pair with no settings at all, which is what a `Date` in a format hole and a `read` at a
   `Date` mean.

   ISO 8601 is the notation, because it is the one every other system accepts and the only one that
   sorts as text in the same order as the dates it names. A program that wants any other format asks
   for it by name - `print("{Date.http(d)}")` - and one that wants this one need not.
-}
instance Show(Date):
    fn show(value: Date, &to: String):
        showWith(Formatted {value: value, style: isoStyle()} :: DateFormat, to)
    fn showBound(value: Date) -> Maybe(Size) =
        showBoundWith(Formatted {value: value, style: isoStyle()} :: DateFormat)

instance Read(Date):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) = readWith(isoStyle(), from, at)

{-
   A duration, read as `duration` writes it - `1h23m4.500s`, and `1.5ms`.

   Each component is a number and the unit that says what it counts, largest first, and a component
   may be missing: `4s` and `1h0m4s` are both readings of durations this writes, and so is `90m`,
   which nothing here writes and which is what a human types.

   **The sub-second units are two letters and the rest are one**, which is the whole of what the
   scaled form added here: `m` is minutes and `ms` is milliseconds, so the letter after the unit is
   what tells them apart and is consumed only where it is an `s`. `us` and `ns` are read the same
   way, and a `µ` is not accepted - the writer does not produce one, for the width reason
   `timeSuffix` states.

   The fraction is a fraction *of its own unit*, scaled by how many digits arrived - so `4.5s` is
   four and a half seconds and `1.5ms` is fifteen hundred microseconds, whether the writer was asked
   for one decimal or three. A fraction of a unit below a microsecond is not a number an `Interval`
   holds and is discarded, which is what `nanos` in `Date.yana` does to the whole count.
-}
instance ReadWith(IntervalStyle, Interval):
  fn readWith(style: IntervalStyle, from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Interval):
    -- `decimals` says how many places are *written* and nothing about how many may be read: a
    -- fraction is read to whatever precision the text carries, so a duration written at three places
    -- and one written at six both read back here. The style is shared; not every field of a shared
    -- style is read by both directions - see `Core/Format/Number.yana`, where `upper` is the same
    -- case.
    let units = length(from)
    let &cursor = at
    let &total = 0 :: I64
    let &any = False

    let &negative = False
    if cursor < units && String.unit(from, cursor) == 45:
        negative = True
        cursor = cursor + 1

    while cursor < units:
        let &value = 0 :: Long
        let &seen = 0 :: Size

        while cursor < units && isDigit(String.unit(from, cursor)) && seen < 18:
            value = value * 10 + (widen(String.unit(from, cursor) - 48) :: Long)
            cursor = cursor + 1
            seen = seen + 1

        if seen == 0 then break

        -- The fraction, which only the seconds have and which is read before the unit letter it
        -- belongs to - so `4.5s` is one component rather than two.
        let &micros = 0 :: I64

        if cursor < units && String.unit(from, cursor) == 46:
            cursor = cursor + 1
            let &places = 0 :: Size

            while cursor < units && isDigit(String.unit(from, cursor)) && places < 6:
                micros = micros * 10 + (widen(String.unit(from, cursor) - 48) :: Long)
                cursor = cursor + 1
                places = places + 1

            let &left = 6 - places
            while left > 0:
                micros = micros * 10
                left = left - 1

        if cursor >= units then return Err(NoDigits)

        let letter = toLowerUnit(String.unit(from, cursor))
        cursor = cursor + 1

        -- `ms`, `us` and `ns` are two units where `d`, `h`, `m` and `s` are one, so a following `s`
        -- is what separates the minutes from the milliseconds. It is consumed only for the three
        -- letters that take one, so `4s` is still one component and `1m30s` is still two.
        if (letter == 109 || letter == 117 || letter == 110)
           && cursor < units && toLowerUnit(String.unit(from, cursor)) == 115:
            cursor = cursor + 1
            any = True

            -- Nanoseconds are below what an `Interval` holds, so the count is divided into
            -- microseconds and the fraction of one is gone - `Date.yana`'s `nanos` exactly.
            total = total + (if:
                letter == 110 -> value / 1000
                letter == 117 -> value
                else          -> value * 1000 + micros / 1000)

            continue

        any = True

        -- The one-letter units, whose multiplier into microseconds is what the fraction is scaled by
        -- as well: `micros` is the fraction in millionths, so a unit worth `M` microseconds
        -- contributes `micros * M / 1000000` of them.
        match letter:
            100 -> total = total + value * 86400000000 + micros * 86400
            104 -> total = total + value * 3600000000 + micros * 3600
            109 -> total = total + value * 60000000 + micros * 60
            115 -> total = total + value * 1000000 + micros
            _ -> return Err(NoDigits)

    if !any then return Err(NoDigits)

    at = cursor
    return Ok(micros(if negative then -total else total))
